WEBVTT

00:00:00.109 --> 00:00:04.035
Dynamic programming solves problems by expressing their answers through

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:08.777
smaller subproblems and storing results that will be needed again Three

00:00:08.777 --> 00:00:14.796
structures occur frequently a position in a sequence a contiguous interval

00:00:15.109 --> 00:00:19.226
and a rooted subtree These structures determine what a state needs

00:00:19.226 --> 00:00:23.125
to remember and which answers must be available before it can be

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:28.207
computed We will examine increasing subsequences and common subsequences

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:33.533
then use interval states to choose parentheses for matrix multiplication

00:00:34.008 --> 00:00:38.750
Finally we will select a maximum weight independent set in a tree

00:00:39.185 --> 00:00:43.750
Each example begins with the meaning of its state The recurrence follows

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:48.668
from the legal choices and the evaluation
order follows from the dependencies

00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:54.443
We will also recover the actual sequence expression or selected vertices

00:00:54.701 --> 00:00:59.117
because the optimal value alone does not identify the solution The

00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:03.682
same questions apply throughout what information does this subproblem

00:01:03.682 --> 00:01:08.859
need which choices cover all valid solutions and why can smaller optimal

00:01:08.872 --> 00:01:09.946
answers be combined

00:01:11.053 --> 00:01:15.509
A complete dynamic programming solution includes a precise state definition

00:01:15.998 --> 00:01:21.053
a recurrence base cases an evaluation order and the location of the

00:01:21.066 --> 00:01:25.170
final answer When the required output is a sequence or a set

00:01:25.237 --> 00:01:30.373
rather than its value the solution also needs reconstruction Correctness

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:34.966
can be established by induction in the dependency order Each transition

00:01:35.102 --> 00:01:40.862
must construct a legal solution and every optimal solution must correspond

00:01:40.862 --> 00:01:46.080
to a choice considered by that transition For matrix chains the final

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.284
multiplication determines the split For a common subsequence the final

00:01:51.284 --> 00:01:56.256
characters determine the two cases For a tree the choice at the

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:00.835
subtree root determines which child states are allowed Chapter fourteen

00:02:00.835 --> 00:02:05.251
of Introduction to Algorithms develops these ideas through rod cutting

00:02:05.727 --> 00:02:10.496
matrix-chain multiplication and longest
common subsequences The company-party

00:02:10.496 --> 00:02:16.664
problem gives a tree application Fibonacci and zero-one knapsack provide

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:21.542
additional examples of how repeated subproblems and state restrictions

00:02:21.759 --> 00:02:23.295
affect the implementation

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:29.451
Dynamic programming is useful when a problem can be expressed using

00:02:29.451 --> 00:02:34.451
smaller subproblems and those subproblems recur Optimal substructure

00:02:34.451 --> 00:02:39.152
means that once a relevant choice is fixed the remaining parts can

00:02:39.166 --> 00:02:44.315
use their own optimal solutions We must justify this property rather

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:48.215
than assume it Overlap means that separate branches of a recursive

00:02:48.215 --> 00:02:53.323
search ask the same question again Saving the answer prevents repeated

00:02:53.323 --> 00:02:58.120
work Compare this with merge sort Its two recursive calls work on

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.399
separate halves and there is normally no repeated request for the same

00:03:02.399 --> 00:03:07.277
half within one run A dynamic programming recurrence often forms a

00:03:07.277 --> 00:03:13.147
directed acyclic graph of dependencies This
connects directly to topological

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:18.364
ordering A state is sufficient only when two situations with that same

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:22.304
state have the same possible future decisions and the same relevant

00:03:22.304 --> 00:03:28.405
outcome If capacity affects future choices for example the state must

00:03:28.418 --> 00:03:33.853
include capacity A table alone does not make an algorithm correct or

00:03:33.853 --> 00:03:34.492
efficient

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:40.961
Consider Fibonacci numbers with zero as the zeroth value and one as

00:03:40.961 --> 00:03:46.369
the first To compute the fifth value recursively we request the fourth

00:03:46.423 --> 00:03:51.640
and third Computing the fourth requests the
third again The same duplication

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:56.409
continues at smaller indices A memoized function checks whether an

00:03:56.409 --> 00:04:01.477
answer already exists before making recursive calls A bottom-up version

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:07.021
fills the values in increasing index order Both compute only a linear

00:04:07.034 --> 00:04:11.708
number of distinct states with constant work per state under a fixed-width

00:04:11.899 --> 00:04:16.749
arithmetic model The naive recursive method takes exponential time

00:04:17.306 --> 00:04:21.450
while these versions take linear time For the fifth value the table

00:04:21.464 --> 00:04:27.673
is zero one one two three five This is a warm-up not

00:04:27.687 --> 00:04:33.380
the main intellectual challenge Fibonacci already gives us the recurrence

00:04:33.801 --> 00:04:37.727
In more interesting problems deciding what the state should mean is

00:04:37.741 --> 00:04:43.094
the difficult part Also remember that fixed-width Java integers eventually

00:04:43.108 --> 00:04:48.760
overflow If we use arbitrary precision numbers arithmetic cost grows

00:04:48.774 --> 00:04:49.901
with the number of bits

00:04:51.026 --> 00:04:55.782
There are two common ways to evaluate the same recurrence Memoization

00:04:55.863 --> 00:05:00.619
keeps a recursive description and stores completed answers It can avoid

00:05:00.619 --> 00:05:04.029
states that are never reached but it needs a call stack and

00:05:04.029 --> 00:05:08.159
a reliable way to distinguish a missing entry from a valid answer

00:05:08.567 --> 00:05:13.159
Zero is often a valid answer so a separate visited flag can

00:05:13.173 --> 00:05:18.200
be useful Tabulation chooses a valid order in advance and fills a

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:23.064
table with loops It avoids recursive call overhead and often makes

00:05:23.078 --> 00:05:29.070
memory compression easier However it may also
compute states that memoization

00:05:29.246 --> 00:05:34.056
never needs Therefore we should not claim that tabulation is always

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:39.151
faster or always uses less memory These are implementation trade-offs

00:05:39.627 --> 00:05:43.730
For either approach correctness depends on the same state definition

00:05:43.852 --> 00:05:48.676
and recurrence Before changing one implementation into the other list

00:05:48.676 --> 00:05:53.214
the dependencies of a state The loop order must make those dependencies

00:05:53.214 --> 00:05:58.132
available For interval problems that usually means increasing interval

00:05:58.146 --> 00:06:02.698
length For a rooted tree it means processing children before their

00:06:02.711 --> 00:06:03.214
parent

00:06:04.322 --> 00:06:10.273
Here is the specification we will use for every example First define

00:06:10.273 --> 00:06:15.219
the state in words including exactly which input elements it covers

00:06:15.423 --> 00:06:20.124
and which restrictions apply Second list the choices that can produce

00:06:20.138 --> 00:06:24.961
a valid solution and turn them into a recurrence Third handle the

00:06:24.974 --> 00:06:30.898
smallest subproblems with explicit base cases Fourth choose an evaluation

00:06:30.898 --> 00:06:36.673
order and explain why every dependency is ready Fifth locate the answer

00:06:36.673 --> 00:06:40.695
to the original problem Sometimes it is the last table entry but

00:06:40.695 --> 00:06:45.858
sometimes it is the maximum over many entries Sixth decide whether

00:06:45.858 --> 00:06:50.654
we need an actual sequence a parenthesization or a set of selected

00:06:50.654 --> 00:06:55.599
nodes These outputs require reconstruction information or an appropriate

00:06:55.599 --> 00:07:01.455
backtracking procedure Finally justify correctness and analyze time

00:07:01.646 --> 00:07:06.306
and memory A useful time estimate is the number of states multiplied

00:07:06.306 --> 00:07:10.042
by the amount of work per state with separate accounting when the

00:07:10.042 --> 00:07:14.472
work varies You should be able to apply this checklist before writing

00:07:14.648 --> 00:07:15.491
any loops

00:07:16.597 --> 00:07:21.203
The three patterns differ mainly in the structure of their states In

00:07:21.203 --> 00:07:27.235
longest increasing subsequence one useful
state records the best subsequence

00:07:27.385 --> 00:07:32.262
ending at a particular position In longest common subsequence the state

00:07:32.276 --> 00:07:36.189
uses a pair of prefix lengths because the answer depends on two

00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:41.148
strings In matrix-chain multiplication the natural question is the

00:07:41.162 --> 00:07:46.773
cheapest way to evaluate a contiguous interval of matrices In tree

00:07:46.787 --> 00:07:51.760
independent set a state refers to a rooted subtree and records whether

00:07:51.773 --> 00:07:56.108
its root is selected Notice that the dimensions of the table come

00:07:56.271 --> 00:08:01.230
from the information the subproblem needs They are not arbitrary choices

00:08:01.678 --> 00:08:06.474
A two-dimensional table can represent two prefixes two interval boundaries

00:08:06.882 --> 00:08:12.303
or a node and a binary condition Those
meanings require different transitions

00:08:12.466 --> 00:08:16.787
and different orders A problem can first be classified by writing its

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.773
state definition Recognizing a familiar pattern helps but the definition

00:08:21.977 --> 00:08:24.491
must still match the exact problem

00:08:25.597 --> 00:08:29.292
Before deriving a recurrence we need to understand the object we are

00:08:29.306 --> 00:08:34.007
choosing A subsequence preserves the order of the original elements

00:08:34.306 --> 00:08:39.849
but it may skip positions A substring uses consecutive positions In

00:08:39.863 --> 00:08:44.021
the string A B C D E A C E is a

00:08:44.021 --> 00:08:49.197
subsequence because we can keep positions one three and five It is

00:08:49.197 --> 00:08:54.374
not a substring because those positions are separated C D is both

00:08:54.374 --> 00:08:59.768
a substring and a subsequence E A is neither because it reverses

00:08:59.768 --> 00:09:05.434
the order This distinction changes algorithms Longest common substring

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:10.760
uses a recurrence that resets a value after a mismatch Longest common

00:09:10.773 --> 00:09:14.632
subsequence can skip a character and keep the best result from a

00:09:14.632 --> 00:09:19.333
neighboring prefix state Ask yourself whether the problem allows gaps

00:09:19.564 --> 00:09:25.067
before choosing a formula For our examples an empty subsequence is

00:09:25.067 --> 00:09:30.135
allowed and repeated letters are distinct positions We match positions

00:09:30.135 --> 00:09:33.980
in increasing order even when their character values are equal

00:09:35.087 --> 00:09:40.141
Let us find a strictly increasing subsequence in the array three one

00:09:40.386 --> 00:09:45.291
two five four We define dp at index i as the length

00:09:45.304 --> 00:09:50.060
of the longest increasing subsequence ending exactly at that index

00:09:50.522 --> 00:09:55.386
The phrase ending exactly here is essential It means the current element

00:09:55.590 --> 00:10:00.807
must belong to the subsequence To extend an earlier solution we choose

00:10:00.821 --> 00:10:05.386
an earlier index j whose value is strictly smaller than the current

00:10:05.386 --> 00:10:10.427
value Appending the current element then adds one to the length stored

00:10:10.427 --> 00:10:15.889
at j We take the best such predecessor If no predecessor works

00:10:16.133 --> 00:10:20.916
the element alone gives length one The final answer is the maximum

00:10:21.052 --> 00:10:26.677
entry of the array because the best subsequence may end anywhere It

00:10:26.677 --> 00:10:31.677
is not automatically the last entry For empty input we return length

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:36.663
zero Strict increase uses a less-than comparison Replacing it with

00:10:36.677 --> 00:10:41.785
less-than-or-equal solves the different problem of longest nondecreasing

00:10:41.785 --> 00:10:46.011
subsequence which treats equal adjacent values differently

00:10:47.125 --> 00:10:51.445
We now evaluate the array from left to right At index zero

00:10:51.907 --> 00:10:57.206
value three has no earlier predecessor so its length is one At

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:02.790
index one value one cannot extend three so its length is also

00:11:02.804 --> 00:11:07.858
one At index two value two can extend one and reaches length

00:11:07.858 --> 00:11:13.633
two At index three value five can extend the subsequence one two

00:11:13.932 --> 00:11:18.782
reaching length three At index four value four can also extend one

00:11:19.231 --> 00:11:24.163
two and reaches length three It cannot extend five because four is

00:11:24.176 --> 00:11:31.133
smaller Our table is therefore one one two three three Several optimal

00:11:31.133 --> 00:11:35.454
answers can exist and a correct implementation may reconstruct either

00:11:35.454 --> 00:11:41.989
one two five or one two four Now predict the result for

00:11:41.989 --> 00:11:48.266
three three three A strictly increasing subsequence has length one

00:11:48.755 --> 00:11:53.293
For a decreasing array the answer is also one provided the array

00:11:53.429 --> 00:11:58.239
is nonempty These are useful tests for the comparison and initialization

00:11:58.239 --> 00:11:58.674
rules

00:11:59.773 --> 00:12:04.555
The length table does not directly contain a chosen subsequence To

00:12:04.555 --> 00:12:09.718
reconstruct one initialize a parent array with minus one Whenever an

00:12:09.718 --> 00:12:15.153
earlier index j improves the best length ending at i store j

00:12:15.384 --> 00:12:19.528
as the parent of i After filling the table find an index

00:12:19.528 --> 00:12:23.686
with maximum length and follow its parent links until the sentinel

00:12:23.686 --> 00:12:28.373
minus one We collect values in reverse order so we reverse the

00:12:28.387 --> 00:12:32.789
collected list before returning it In our example choosing the last

00:12:32.803 --> 00:12:39.351
maximum gives indices four two one in reverse Their values become one

00:12:39.895 --> 00:12:44.582
two four after reversal Different tie rules may produce a different

00:12:44.596 --> 00:12:49.718
optimal subsequence so tests should normally check its length increasing

00:12:49.718 --> 00:12:53.645
order and whether it is a subsequence of the input The double

00:12:53.645 --> 00:12:58.523
loop takes quadratic time and the two arrays use linear memory A

00:12:58.536 --> 00:13:02.707
binary search method reduces the time to n log n but its

00:13:02.707 --> 00:13:04.515
tails array has a different meaning

00:13:05.629 --> 00:13:10.778
Longest common subsequence compares two sequences Our first string

00:13:10.928 --> 00:13:14.963
is A B C D E and the second is A C

00:13:14.977 --> 00:13:19.093
E We define L at i j as the length of a

00:13:19.107 --> 00:13:23.332
longest common subsequence between the first i characters of A and

00:13:23.346 --> 00:13:28.224
the first j characters of B We use half-open prefix notation so

00:13:28.224 --> 00:13:32.640
a prefix of length i ends just before index i This makes

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:38.183
the empty prefix natural The table has one extra row and one

00:13:38.373 --> 00:13:44.555
extra column Row zero and column zero contain zeros because an empty

00:13:44.555 --> 00:13:48.971
sequence has no nonempty common subsequence with any other sequence

00:13:49.419 --> 00:13:53.631
The original problem is the table entry at the two full lengths

00:13:54.066 --> 00:13:57.436
We are not requiring the current last characters to appear in the

00:13:57.436 --> 00:14:01.607
answer which is different from our LIS state Keep this difference in

00:14:01.607 --> 00:14:06.675
mind when locating the result In this simple example A C E

00:14:06.675 --> 00:14:09.854
is common to both strings and has length three

00:14:10.957 --> 00:14:15.576
To derive the LCS recurrence compare the last characters of the two

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:21.310
prefixes If they are equal we can extend an optimal common subsequence

00:14:21.324 --> 00:14:25.617
of the two shorter prefixes by this matching character The value is

00:14:25.617 --> 00:14:31.038
one plus the diagonal entry If the characters differ a common subsequence

00:14:31.052 --> 00:14:35.454
cannot use both as its final matching pair At least one of

00:14:35.454 --> 00:14:39.788
these final positions is unused We therefore consider skipping the

00:14:39.788 --> 00:14:44.082
last position of A or skipping the last position of B and

00:14:44.095 --> 00:14:48.688
take the larger answer These choices cover all possibilities for an

00:14:48.701 --> 00:14:54.503
optimal solution The base cases handle either empty prefix A row-by-row

00:14:54.503 --> 00:15:00.101
evaluation from small indices to large indices makes the diagonal above

00:15:00.576 --> 00:15:05.223
and left dependencies available Notice that a mismatch does not reset

00:15:05.237 --> 00:15:10.359
the answer to zero Resetting would discard useful subsequences containing

00:15:10.372 --> 00:15:16.052
earlier matches That rule belongs to the longest common substring problem

00:15:16.487 --> 00:15:21.133
where consecutive positions are required Write the state sentence beside

00:15:21.133 --> 00:15:24.082
the recurrence to keep these problems separate

00:15:25.175 --> 00:15:28.857
Let us fill the complete table for our two strings The first

00:15:28.857 --> 00:15:34.075
row and first column are zero Comparing A with A gives one

00:15:34.618 --> 00:15:39.156
and that value remains available as the second prefix grows On the

00:15:39.156 --> 00:15:43.748
row for B no new character matches the current characters of A

00:15:43.762 --> 00:15:47.743
C E so the best value stays one On the row for

00:15:47.743 --> 00:15:52.322
C and column for C we add one to the diagonal value

00:15:52.322 --> 00:15:56.941
one obtaining two At the next column we keep that value even

00:15:56.941 --> 00:16:01.276
though C and E differ The row for D also preserves the

00:16:01.289 --> 00:16:07.091
best length two Finally E matches E and extends the diagonal length

00:16:07.104 --> 00:16:12.118
two to three The bottom-right entry is three Before looking at the

00:16:12.118 --> 00:16:16.493
finished row predict the value at the D row and E column

00:16:16.941 --> 00:16:22.498
It is two representing the common subsequence A C This table is

00:16:22.498 --> 00:16:27.662
a record of prefix questions so every entry has a concrete interpretation

00:16:27.675 --> 00:16:28.803
beyond its number

00:16:29.893 --> 00:16:34.879
To recover a longest common subsequence start at the bottom-right corner

00:16:34.893 --> 00:16:39.390
of the full table When the two current characters match record that

00:16:39.404 --> 00:16:43.874
character and move diagonally to the shorter prefixes When they do

00:16:43.887 --> 00:16:48.072
not match move toward an above or left neighbor that retains the

00:16:48.167 --> 00:16:53.643
optimal value Continue until one prefix becomes empty For our example

00:16:54.105 --> 00:17:00.300
we record E later C and finally A Reversing these characters gives

00:17:00.491 --> 00:17:04.281
A C E A tie between the above and left values means

00:17:04.295 --> 00:17:09.512
that more than one optimal route may exist A deterministic implementation

00:17:09.689 --> 00:17:13.629
can prefer one direction but it should not claim that its resulting

00:17:13.629 --> 00:17:18.914
string is the only answer The classic textbook example A B C

00:17:18.928 --> 00:17:23.276
B D A B and B D C A B A has

00:17:23.344 --> 00:17:27.732
optimal length four and several possible answers If we compress the

00:17:27.746 --> 00:17:32.773
table to two rows the length is still available but this simple

00:17:32.787 --> 00:17:37.950
backtracking procedure loses information Reconstruction requires retaining

00:17:37.963 --> 00:17:40.246
decisions or using a different method

00:17:41.365 --> 00:17:46.827
This Java fragment directly implements the prefix definition We allocate

00:17:47.221 --> 00:17:53.634
n plus one rows and m plus one columns Java initializes primitive

00:17:53.647 --> 00:17:58.946
integer arrays to zero giving the required boundary conditions The

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:03.824
loops begin at one because row zero and column zero already represent

00:18:03.946 --> 00:18:09.259
empty prefixes To access a character subtract one from its prefix length

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:14.612
On a match use the diagonal value plus one Otherwise take the

00:18:14.612 --> 00:18:19.558
maximum from above and left The answer is L at n m

00:18:19.992 --> 00:18:24.517
Each table cell uses constant work so there are order n times

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:29.136
m operations and the full table uses the same order of memory

00:18:29.571 --> 00:18:32.751
If we only need the length we can keep the previous row

00:18:32.873 --> 00:18:37.506
and current row giving linear memory in the shorter string length That

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:42.492
optimization should follow a correct full-table implementation These

00:18:42.492 --> 00:18:48.403
examples use Latin characters Java char values are UTF-sixteen code

00:18:48.403 --> 00:18:53.729
units so a general Unicode application may require code-point processing

00:18:54.829 --> 00:19:00.005
Related sequence problems include edit distance and longest palindromic

00:19:00.005 --> 00:19:05.712
subsequence For edit distance with unit costs an empty prefix needs

00:19:05.725 --> 00:19:09.951
one insertion per character of the other prefix The boundary values

00:19:09.951 --> 00:19:15.793
are therefore i and j not all zeros Each interior state considers

00:19:15.807 --> 00:19:21.649
deletion insertion and replacement or matching For longest palindromic

00:19:21.649 --> 00:19:26.812
subsequence the state refers to an interval within one string A single

00:19:26.812 --> 00:19:32.519
character has length one and an empty interval has length zero Matching

00:19:32.532 --> 00:19:37.587
endpoints can contribute two plus the interior answer Different endpoints

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:42.614
require skipping one side and taking the maximum This example also

00:19:42.614 --> 00:19:47.247
shows that pattern categories overlap A problem about a subsequence

00:19:47.451 --> 00:19:51.921
can use interval states We choose the state according to dependencies

00:19:52.369 --> 00:19:56.541
not according to one keyword in the problem title In each new

00:19:56.541 --> 00:20:01.323
problem derive the bases again instead of copying them from LCS

00:20:02.413 --> 00:20:07.616
Zero-one knapsack gives another example of choosing or skipping Suppose

00:20:07.616 --> 00:20:12.915
the weights are two three and four the values are three four

00:20:13.146 --> 00:20:18.106
and five and the capacity is five Selecting the first two items

00:20:18.214 --> 00:20:23.432
gives weight five and value seven Define K at i c as

00:20:23.445 --> 00:20:27.426
the maximum total value using the first i items with total weight

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:30.918
at most c We can skip the current item and keep the

00:20:30.918 --> 00:20:35.674
previous row at capacity c If it fits we can take it

00:20:36.108 --> 00:20:40.198
and add its value to the previous row at the remaining capacity

00:20:40.633 --> 00:20:46.271
Using the previous row prevents selecting the same item again The zero-item

00:20:46.271 --> 00:20:51.203
row is zero when selecting nothing is allowed With positive integer

00:20:51.203 --> 00:20:57.059
weights the time is order n times capacity This is pseudopolynomial

00:20:57.372 --> 00:21:01.394
because capacity can be much larger than the number of bits used

00:21:01.394 --> 00:21:06.475
to write it Dynamic programming does not automatically guarantee polynomial

00:21:06.475 --> 00:21:08.445
time in input length

00:21:09.541 --> 00:21:14.418
Now consider memory compression for zero-one knapsack If we store only

00:21:14.418 --> 00:21:19.622
one array we must ensure that the remaining-capacity value still refers

00:21:19.622 --> 00:21:24.731
to the previous item stage Processing capacities from high to low achieves

00:21:24.731 --> 00:21:28.725
this A smaller capacity has not yet been updated for the current

00:21:28.725 --> 00:21:33.698
item so reading it cannot reuse that item If we process capacities

00:21:33.698 --> 00:21:38.372
upward we may read a value already improved by the same item

00:21:38.889 --> 00:21:43.263
accidentally implementing repeated selection A tiny counterexample

00:21:43.522 --> 00:21:48.291
makes the issue visible Take one item of weight two and value

00:21:48.304 --> 00:21:53.522
three with capacity four The zero-one optimum is three because we have

00:21:53.658 --> 00:21:58.753
only one item An ascending loop first writes three at capacity two

00:21:59.323 --> 00:22:03.494
then reads that new value when it reaches capacity four and produces

00:22:03.508 --> 00:22:08.766
six That is wrong for zero-one knapsack The same issue occurs in

00:22:08.821 --> 00:22:13.168
other compressed recurrences memory compression changes which time

00:22:13.168 --> 00:22:17.258
stage a table entry represents so loop direction becomes part of the

00:22:17.272 --> 00:22:18.331
correctness argument

00:22:19.453 --> 00:22:24.901
Our next pattern chooses a split inside an interval Matrix multiplication

00:22:25.023 --> 00:22:29.820
is associative so different parenthesizations give the same mathematical

00:22:29.833 --> 00:22:34.439
product provided the dimensions are compatible However intermediate

00:22:34.439 --> 00:22:39.602
matrix sizes can change the number of scalar multiplications dramatically

00:22:40.051 --> 00:22:44.371
Multiplying a matrix with p rows and q columns by one with

00:22:44.371 --> 00:22:48.882
q rows and r columns costs p times q times r scalar

00:22:48.896 --> 00:22:53.434
multiplications using the standard algorithm Matrix-chain optimization

00:22:53.868 --> 00:22:58.461
chooses parentheses while preserving the original matrix order It does

00:22:58.461 --> 00:23:02.999
not reorder the matrices and it does not compute the matrix entries

00:23:02.999 --> 00:23:08.148
themselves Think of it as planning a computation before executing it

00:23:08.583 --> 00:23:12.727
The output can be a minimum cost and an expression describing the

00:23:12.741 --> 00:23:17.347
order of operations We will start with three matrices where there are

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:22.578
just two parenthesizations then generalize to an interval state The

00:23:22.591 --> 00:23:27.374
final multiplication always splits the chain into a left contiguous

00:23:27.374 --> 00:23:29.711
part and a right contiguous part

00:23:30.791 --> 00:23:34.799
Let us calculate both possibilities rather than accept the answer on

00:23:34.799 --> 00:23:41.375
faith For open parenthesis A B close parenthesis C the first multiplication

00:23:41.389 --> 00:23:46.620
costs ten times thirty times five which is fifteen hundred Its result

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:52.354
has ten rows and five columns Multiplying that result by C costs

00:23:52.354 --> 00:23:57.476
ten times five times sixty which is three thousand The total is

00:23:57.489 --> 00:24:02.965
forty-five hundred For A open parenthesis B C close parenthesis the

00:24:02.965 --> 00:24:07.870
first multiplication costs thirty times five times sixty which is nine

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:12.911
thousand and creates a thirty by sixty matrix Multiplying A by that

00:24:12.924 --> 00:24:18.604
matrix costs ten times thirty times sixty or eighteen thousand The

00:24:18.604 --> 00:24:23.114
total is twenty-seven thousand Both expressions have the same final

00:24:23.114 --> 00:24:29.256
dimensions but one uses six times as many scalar multiplications Before

00:24:29.256 --> 00:24:34.392
moving on identify the dimensions of each intermediate result yourself

00:24:34.826 --> 00:24:38.522
That check explains why the transition cost depends on the two interval

00:24:38.522 --> 00:24:40.492
boundaries and the chosen split

00:24:41.581 --> 00:24:47.070
Use one-based matrix indices and an array of dimensions p Matrix i

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:51.744
has dimensions p at i minus one by p at i. Define M

00:24:51.744 --> 00:24:56.784
at i j as the minimum cost of multiplying matrices i through

00:24:56.784 --> 00:25:02.844
j In the final operation some split k separates matrices i through

00:25:02.844 --> 00:25:07.831
k from matrices k plus one through j The left and right

00:25:07.831 --> 00:25:13.496
parts must themselves use optimal parenthesizations otherwise replacing

00:25:13.496 --> 00:25:17.804
a more expensive part would improve the full expression Their result

00:25:17.804 --> 00:25:21.961
dimensions are p at i minus one by p at k and

00:25:21.961 --> 00:25:25.901
p at k by p at j Combining them therefore costs the

00:25:25.901 --> 00:25:30.195
product of these three dimensions We try every k from i through

00:25:30.331 --> 00:25:34.543
j minus one and choose the minimum total The base case M

00:25:34.543 --> 00:25:40.317
at i i is zero We compute shorter intervals first With quadratic

00:25:40.317 --> 00:25:44.787
many intervals and up to linear many splits each the time is

00:25:44.787 --> 00:25:47.083
cubic and memory is quadratic

00:25:48.167 --> 00:25:53.942
Consider dimensions forty twenty thirty ten thirty These define four

00:25:53.942 --> 00:25:59.077
matrices First solve intervals of length two Their costs are twenty-four

00:25:59.077 --> 00:26:05.028
thousand six thousand and nine thousand For matrices one through three

00:26:05.504 --> 00:26:09.906
splitting after the first costs fourteen thousand while splitting after

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:14.716
the second costs thirty-six thousand We keep fourteen thousand For

00:26:14.716 --> 00:26:20.749
matrices two through four the best cost is twelve thousand Now consider

00:26:20.749 --> 00:26:25.667
the complete chain A final split after matrix one costs thirty-six

00:26:25.667 --> 00:26:31.632
thousand A split after matrix two costs sixty-nine thousand A split

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:36.740
after matrix three costs twenty-six thousand so it is the best To

00:26:36.754 --> 00:26:41.673
reconstruct the expression retain that split and recursively reconstruct

00:26:41.740 --> 00:26:46.618
both sides The first three matrices use A one multiplied by the

00:26:46.632 --> 00:26:51.333
product of A two and A three Then multiply by A four

00:26:51.768 --> 00:26:57.012
The resulting expression has total cost
twenty-six thousand These intermediate

00:26:57.026 --> 00:27:01.659
values are useful for detecting dimension-index errors in an implementation

00:27:02.759 --> 00:27:07.623
The loop structure should express the dependency order visibly The

00:27:07.623 --> 00:27:11.360
outer loop grows the interval length from two to the number of

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:17.949
matrices For each start index i compute the end index j Initialize

00:27:17.949 --> 00:27:22.379
the interval cost to a large value then try every split k

00:27:22.379 --> 00:27:28.262
inside it The diagonal entries remain zero representing a single matrix

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:33.615
The fragment assumes positive compatible
dimensions and that all intermediate

00:27:33.615 --> 00:27:38.846
costs fit a Java long Writing one L before the product promotes

00:27:38.846 --> 00:27:43.737
the arithmetic before multiplication preventing an accidental integer

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:48.819
overflow in that expression It does not protect against values exceeding

00:27:48.819 --> 00:27:53.860
the long range so constraints still matter A split array can record

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:58.873
the k whenever a candidate improves the current minimum A reconstruction

00:27:58.873 --> 00:28:03.411
procedure then returns a matrix name for a single-element interval

00:28:03.683 --> 00:28:08.099
and combines recursively reconstructed left and right expressions otherwise

00:28:08.547 --> 00:28:13.887
Iterating intervals by arbitrary start and end indices without considering

00:28:13.887 --> 00:28:17.501
length can read values that have not yet been computed

00:28:18.589 --> 00:28:22.407
Another interval problem asks for the minimum cost to cut a stick

00:28:22.733 --> 00:28:27.352
at specified positions A cut costs the length of the segment being

00:28:27.352 --> 00:28:32.828
cut at that moment The order matters because later cuts may operate

00:28:32.841 --> 00:28:38.684
on shorter segments Add the two endpoints to the sorted required positions

00:28:39.119 --> 00:28:43.222
Define C at l r as the minimum cost of completing all

00:28:43.235 --> 00:28:48.018
required cuts strictly inside that interval If the endpoint indices

00:28:48.018 --> 00:28:52.135
are adjacent there is no internal cut and the cost is zero

00:28:52.556 --> 00:28:58.004
Otherwise try each internal required position as the first cut It costs

00:28:58.004 --> 00:29:02.461
the current segment length plus the optimal costs of the left and

00:29:02.461 --> 00:29:06.999
right intervals For a stick of length ten with required cuts at

00:29:06.999 --> 00:29:12.162
four and seven cutting at four first costs ten plus six giving

00:29:12.176 --> 00:29:17.964
sixteen Cutting at seven first costs ten plus seven giving seventeen

00:29:18.385 --> 00:29:23.059
In this example the interval pattern is familiar but the combine cost

00:29:23.168 --> 00:29:25.165
differs from matrix multiplication

00:29:26.255 --> 00:29:30.902
We now move from intervals to a hierarchy Imagine a company party

00:29:31.024 --> 00:29:35.141
where each employee has a contribution to the event represented by

00:29:35.141 --> 00:29:40.168
a weight An employee and their immediate supervisor cannot both attend

00:29:40.617 --> 00:29:44.625
The supervisor relation forms a tree which is the setting of the

00:29:44.638 --> 00:29:49.367
textbook's company-party problem In graph language we want a maximum

00:29:49.367 --> 00:29:54.149
weight independent set of a tree Independent means that no edge has

00:29:54.163 --> 00:29:59.543
both endpoints selected A locally attractive employee is not necessarily

00:29:59.557 --> 00:30:04.638
part of the optimal global set because choosing that employee can exclude

00:30:04.652 --> 00:30:09.883
valuable children We root the tree and define subproblems for its subtrees

00:30:10.318 --> 00:30:14.407
The key boundary information is whether the subtree's root is selected

00:30:14.842 --> 00:30:19.434
That binary decision tells the parent what combinations are legal Our

00:30:19.448 --> 00:30:24.299
worked example has root zero with weight five children one and two

00:30:24.326 --> 00:30:28.035
with weights three and four and nodes three and four below node

00:30:28.035 --> 00:30:31.894
one with weights six and one We allow selecting no nodes

00:30:33.026 --> 00:30:37.673
Define take at u as the maximum total weight in u's subtree

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:41.803
when u must be selected Define skip at u as the maximum

00:30:41.994 --> 00:30:45.934
when u must be excluded If we select u every child must

00:30:45.934 --> 00:30:50.485
be excluded so we add the child's skip value If we exclude

00:30:50.486 --> 00:30:55.146
u each child may be included or excluded so we add the

00:30:55.146 --> 00:30:59.847
larger of its two states For a leaf take equals its weight

00:31:00.159 --> 00:31:05.839
and skip equals zero After processing children before parents the answer

00:31:06.206 --> 00:31:11.178
is the larger root state The two states are necessary because one

00:31:11.342 --> 00:31:15.649
unconstrained best value would hide whether the child root was selected

00:31:16.111 --> 00:31:20.431
The parent needs that information to enforce the edge restriction On

00:31:20.431 --> 00:31:25.227
a tree separate child subtrees have no edges between them so their

00:31:25.241 --> 00:31:29.371
legal choices can be combined independently once the decision at u

00:31:29.371 --> 00:31:34.480
is fixed That independence is the reason the recurrence works It does

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:39.942
not automatically extend to a general graph containing cycles or cross

00:31:39.956 --> 00:31:40.499
edges

00:31:41.591 --> 00:31:45.613
Start with the leaves Node two has take four and skip zero

00:31:46.048 --> 00:31:50.558
Node three has take six and skip zero Node four has take

00:31:50.558 --> 00:31:55.029
one and skip zero At node one taking the node gives its

00:31:55.164 --> 00:32:00.110
own weight three plus zero from each excluded child Skipping it lets

00:32:00.110 --> 00:32:05.287
us take both children giving six plus one or seven Now compute

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:09.838
root zero Taking the root gives five plus skip one which is

00:32:09.852 --> 00:32:15.694
seven plus skip two which is zero The result is twelve Skipping

00:32:15.694 --> 00:32:19.961
the root gives the best state of node one seven plus the

00:32:19.974 --> 00:32:24.648
best state of node two four The result is eleven Therefore the

00:32:24.662 --> 00:32:31.170
optimum is twelve One selected set is nodes zero three and four

00:32:31.618 --> 00:32:36.007
They are pairwise nonadjacent and their weights sum to twelve Notice

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:41.075
that a selected grandparent and selected grandchild are permitted The

00:32:41.075 --> 00:32:46.958
rule prohibits adjacent endpoints not every pair related anywhere in

00:32:46.958 --> 00:32:47.773
the hierarchy

00:32:48.863 --> 00:32:52.817
Computing the optimum weight is only part of the tree problem when

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:57.980
a guest list is requested Reconstruction starts at the root with no

00:32:57.994 --> 00:33:02.219
selected parent If the parent of a node is selected the node

00:33:02.341 --> 00:33:07.450
must be skipped Otherwise compare its take and skip values and choose

00:33:07.464 --> 00:33:11.594
a better state using a documented tie rule if they are equal

00:33:12.029 --> 00:33:16.662
Pass the resulting decision to each child In our example the root

00:33:16.662 --> 00:33:21.825
is selected because twelve exceeds eleven Its children one and two

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:26.553
must therefore be skipped Since node one is skipped nodes three and

00:33:26.567 --> 00:33:30.901
four are free to take their better states and both are selected

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:36.974
The result is zero three four Validation should check two independent

00:33:36.975 --> 00:33:42.056
properties every selected pair is nonadjacent and the sum of their

00:33:42.056 --> 00:33:46.241
weights equals the reported optimum A correct weight with an illegal

00:33:46.241 --> 00:33:52.518
guest list is still an incorrect reconstructed solution For tiny trees

00:33:52.926 --> 00:33:58.428
exhaustive enumeration of all vertex subsets provides a useful independent

00:33:58.428 --> 00:33:59.053
reference

00:34:00.143 --> 00:34:05.102
A recursive depth-first traversal naturally computes children before

00:34:05.102 --> 00:34:09.600
their parent if we combine results after recursive calls On a very

00:34:09.613 --> 00:34:15.646
deep tree however recursion can exceed Java's call-stack limit An iterative

00:34:15.646 --> 00:34:21.053
alternative first roots the validated tree and records an order in

00:34:21.053 --> 00:34:25.265
which every parent appears before its children Reversing that order

00:34:25.279 --> 00:34:30.157
puts all children before their parent The displayed fragment assumes

00:34:30.157 --> 00:34:36.502
that this traversal and parent array already exist For each node initialize

00:34:36.502 --> 00:34:41.135
take to its weight and skip to zero Then combine only neighbors

00:34:41.135 --> 00:34:45.999
whose parent is the current node Each edge contributes constant work

00:34:46.162 --> 00:34:50.591
to the appropriate parent-child combination giving linear time for

00:34:50.605 --> 00:34:56.719
the whole tree The adjacency list parent array order and state arrays

00:34:56.719 --> 00:35:01.189
use linear memory If the input is an arbitrary graph we must

00:35:01.203 --> 00:35:05.877
validate connectivity and the tree property rather than silently treating

00:35:05.877 --> 00:35:10.795
a spanning tree as the original problem Discarding extra edges can

00:35:10.795 --> 00:35:15.021
produce an invalid independent set for the original graph

00:35:16.141 --> 00:35:20.937
All our examples share one correctness structure Establish the base

00:35:20.950 --> 00:35:25.760
cases Assume that the smaller states already have their intended optimal

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:31.494
values Show that every transition constructs a legal solution and that

00:35:31.671 --> 00:35:36.304
every optimal solution has a final choice represented among the candidates

00:35:36.752 --> 00:35:41.086
Taking the best candidate then gives the desired optimal value The

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:47.214
evaluation order supports this induction For complexity LIS has linear

00:35:47.214 --> 00:35:52.350
many end-index states but examines up to linear many predecessors for

00:35:52.472 --> 00:35:57.431
each giving quadratic time LCS has a product of the two prefix

00:35:57.445 --> 00:36:02.907
counts and constant work per state Matrix chains have quadratic many

00:36:02.921 --> 00:36:08.233
intervals and up to linear many split positions Tree DP has two

00:36:08.247 --> 00:36:12.649
states per node and total combination work proportional to the tree

00:36:12.649 --> 00:36:17.309
edges Memory depends on the representation and whether reconstruction

00:36:17.418 --> 00:36:22.431
is required The length-only LCS optimization does not preserve the

00:36:22.431 --> 00:36:26.888
full table used in our simple backtracking method Always state what

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:31.168
output the memory bound supports rather than presenting a compressed

00:36:31.168 --> 00:36:33.219
bound without that qualification

00:36:34.333 --> 00:36:38.735
Testing should challenge the state definition not just repeat the main

00:36:38.748 --> 00:36:45.515
example For LIS test an empty array one value all equal values

00:36:45.773 --> 00:36:52.349
and a decreasing array For LCS test either empty string identical strings

00:36:52.634 --> 00:36:56.982
no common character and repeated characters with several optimal answers

00:36:57.444 --> 00:37:03.273
For matrix chains a single matrix must cost zero Three matrices let

00:37:03.273 --> 00:37:08.504
us enumerate both possible parenthesizations by hand and a four-matrix

00:37:08.504 --> 00:37:14.034
case exposes mistakes in interval order For tree independent set test

00:37:14.115 --> 00:37:19.156
a single node a chain a star and negative weights when selecting

00:37:19.156 --> 00:37:25.270
nothing is allowed Compare small random instances with a simple exhaustive

00:37:25.270 --> 00:37:30.936
solver Reconstructed outputs need separate checks for feasibility and

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:35.705
objective value Also distinguish an unknown state from a valid zero

00:37:35.705 --> 00:37:40.746
answer and avoid adding costs to an infinity sentinel Before attempting

00:37:40.746 --> 00:37:45.216
memory optimization make the straightforward version correct and inspect

00:37:45.216 --> 00:37:50.134
its table A printed table for a five-element example often explains

00:37:50.257 --> 00:37:53.803
a failure more clearly than a large performance test

00:37:54.925 --> 00:37:58.648
The state definition determines where the final answer is found In

00:37:58.648 --> 00:38:04.585
longest increasing subsequence the state requires the subsequence to

00:38:04.762 --> 00:38:10.373
end at a particular index The full optimum can end anywhere so

00:38:10.373 --> 00:38:15.631
we take the maximum across indices In longest common subsequence the

00:38:15.631 --> 00:38:20.726
state already allows arbitrary common subsequences of two prefixes

00:38:21.175 --> 00:38:25.156
The answer for the full strings is therefore the entry for both

00:38:25.156 --> 00:38:30.509
complete prefixes On a mismatch skipping one final character preserves

00:38:30.577 --> 00:38:34.476
earlier matches so the recurrence takes the best neighboring prefix

00:38:34.476 --> 00:38:39.707
value Matrix-chain states depend on shorter intervals which explains

00:38:39.707 --> 00:38:44.694
the increasing-length order In a tree the parent needs to know whether

00:38:44.694 --> 00:38:49.639
the child root is selected take and skip preserve this boundary information

00:38:50.074 --> 00:38:54.354
Reconstruction follows the decisions that attained the optimum Its

00:38:54.368 --> 00:38:59.245
output must be checked for feasibility as well as objective value These

00:38:59.259 --> 00:39:03.335
distinctions are useful when adapting a recurrence to a new problem

00:39:03.688 --> 00:39:06.691
rather than copying a formula unchanged

00:39:07.813 --> 00:39:12.500
The examples illustrate the same design
process under different dependencies

00:39:12.949 --> 00:39:18.302
Sequence states describe an ending position or a pair of prefixes Interval

00:39:18.302 --> 00:39:23.723
states describe a contiguous range and consider a split Tree states

00:39:23.723 --> 00:39:28.696
describe a subtree together with a condition at its root The recurrence

00:39:28.696 --> 00:39:33.397
specifies how the smaller answers combine while the base cases handle

00:39:33.397 --> 00:39:38.614
the smallest valid inputs A suitable order makes every required answer

00:39:38.628 --> 00:39:42.758
available Complexity follows from the number of states and the work

00:39:42.758 --> 00:39:47.690
spent evaluating their choices Memory reductions must preserve the

00:39:47.704 --> 00:39:52.677
information required by the requested output In particular obtaining

00:39:52.677 --> 00:39:57.351
an optimal value and reconstructing a solution are separate requirements

00:39:57.799 --> 00:40:01.671
The references include the dynamic programming chapter of Introduction

00:40:01.739 --> 00:40:06.902
to Algorithms and additional explanations of subsequences matrix chains

00:40:07.147 --> 00:40:12.473
and independent sets To review a solution state its subproblem in one

00:40:12.473 --> 00:40:17.174
sentence derive its choices and check a small example by hand before

00:40:17.188 --> 00:40:18.669
considering an optimization
